+---
+title: Adding a built-in ParamType (click.Duration)
+author: HarzerHeribert
+date: 2026-10-15
+cost: { minutes: 2, tokens: 49504 }
+touches:
+  - CHANGES.md
+  - docs/api.md
+  - docs/parameter-types.md
+  - src/click/__init__.py
+  - src/click/types.py
+  - tests/test_types.py
+  - tests/test_imports.py
+---
+
+## Decided
+- New param types live in src/click/types.py next to their closest sibling (Duration follows DateTime), get a `__repr__` returning the class name, and are re-exported in src/click/__init__.py as `from .types import X as X`.
+- Duration accepts only timedelta (passthrough) or str; non-str values (including int defaults) fail, per the spec. Parsing uses `[0-9]` + `re.fullmatch` (not `\d`/`$`) so Unicode digits and trailing newlines are rejected; OverflowError/ValueError from timedelta/int are routed to self.fail.
+- Docs for a new type go in three places: a section + entry in "Built-in Types Listing" in docs/parameter-types.md, an autoclass in docs/api.md, and a CHANGES.md entry (top released section was 8.5.0, so a "Version 8.6.0 / Unreleased" section was added).
+
+## Tried and ruled out
+- Nothing notable; the DateTime pattern transferred directly.
+
+## Watch out
+- tests/test_imports.py enforces an ALLOWED_IMPORTS set for `import click`; any new stdlib import in types.py must be on it (`re` and `datetime` already are).
+- The metavar comes from `type.name.upper()` via the default ParamType.get_metavar returning None; only override get_metavar if the help text should differ.
+- ruff/mypy are not installed in the bench venv; check the 88-column limit manually (types.py line ~418 was already over before this change).
