+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours),
+`m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once and units must be given in that order, without
+spaces. Units are case-insensitive, so `1h30m`, `90m`, `1H30M`, and `5400` all mean the same duration. Negative
+numbers and decimals are not accepted.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
